WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.419
Film Courage: In what way can a screenwriter
ruin a scene?

00:00:03.419 --> 00:00:08.440
Adam Skelter: It’s almost easier to ask
what way they can fix it because there’s

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:09.950
so many many ways to ruin it.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.510
I mean there’s lots of ways to do something
badly, you know?

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:20.529
Some of the most typical ways are just exposition,
just using the straight I’m going to explain

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:22.449
exactly what I want.

00:00:22.449 --> 00:00:32.470
Often if the dialogue is directly proportionate
to the desires like somebody that walks around

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:35.610
and says I want this glass of water.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:37.620
I want to shake your hand…I want.”

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:47.770
You know when their action is directly proportionate
to what they are saying it tends to get bad,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:53.410
you’re not revealing anything to the character
and you’re not exposing, you’re not emotionally

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:54.410
engaging.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:59.600
Although there was a really exception to that.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.560
Did you see THE SACRED DEER?

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.370
Film Courage: No I wanted to!

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.700
I was watching the trailer and I think I missed
the window at The Laemmle and I wanted to

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.700
see it.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.950
Adam: It’s so…I loved it!

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:16.719
He’s an excellent filmmaker and he did this
thing where he is breaking all the rules (there’s

00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:19.969
no rules whatever).

00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:24.240
But this assumption of having characters say
exactly what they want, exactly what is on

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:32.359
their mind and delivering it in this kind
of deadpan way and it does it in such a way

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:35.341
that is incredibly intriguing and I still
can’t wrap my brain around it.

00:01:35.341 --> 00:01:40.030
I’m still trying to understand why it was
so effective but I couldn’t take my eyes

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:41.579
off of it.

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:46.360
It’s Colin Farrell walking around with this
kid [actors Barry Keoghan] talking about the

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.850
importance of watches and the importance the
importance of “I think I should get a new

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.759
wristband and it’s important.”

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:56.860
And they are very matter of fact and it feels
just left of normal conversation, like it

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:03.850
doesn’t…it feels very contrived, it’s
stilted and it’s for some reason you just

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:08.729
slowly get drawn into this kind of rhythm
and cadence and before you know it you’re

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:15.989
like “Oh, these people, they are not talking
about their watches, they are talking about

00:02:15.989 --> 00:02:19.370
what their relationships actually mean to
each other and it’s so well done.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:20.370
I loved it!

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.120
Film Courage: I really want to see that one.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.580
Adam: It’s really darkly funny.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.500
Like there are a few movies this last year
that they felt like these kind of dark dramas

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.500
but they were so funny, they were written
in this great absurdist way.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.220
Like MOTHER, I thought MOTHER was hilarious.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.970
Film Courage: I didn’t get to see that.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.230
It looks really intriguing because you couldn’t
really figure out from the trailer what it

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.319
was about, it wasn’t clear.

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:48.110
Adam: I’m actually planning a video where
I’m talking about allegory and I think MOTHER

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:52.680
is much more interesting than a lot of people
are giving it credit for just because of…it’s

00:02:52.680 --> 00:03:00.530
cool to see a filmmaker who is deliberately
engaging allegory and allegorical tropes in

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.370
the way that very few are so it will be fun,
that will be forthcoming, it will be a tease.

